Like the others said: you won't notice a -7 to-hit penalty except in
the early game.

To illustrate, I finished ascending a barbarian yesterday
two-weaponing +7 Fire Brand and a +7 silver saber.  The barbarian was
"only" level 17, but I was really hitting just about anything I
engaged.  If I read the spoiler correctly, my to-hit calculation would
have been based on:

        17 for experience level
       +13 for maxed out luck
        +4 for 18 dexterity
        +1 for attacking directly
        +3 for 25 strength
        +7 for the +7 weapon enchantment
        -7 for basic two-weaponing skill
       ---
        38

possibly plus or minus a few extra bonuses and penalties.  That
number's added to the monster's armor class, and d20 is rolled against
it, so I should have had a guaranteed hit (with both weapons) against
any monster with an armor class >= -18.

In fact, you said you two-weaponed Stormbringer and a silver saber as
a rogue in a previous game.  The two-weapon to-hit penalty is based on
the *lower* of your two-weaponing skill and your skill in your primary
weapon, so even if your rogue was expert in two-weaponing, he was at
best skilled in broadsword, so he was subject to a to-hit penalty of
-5 anyway, and you're really only looking at a 2-point difference from
that.

